{# Site footer.

   The links come from settings.footer_nav, NOT settings.nav: the bar at the
   top carries the places a reader is going, this carries the fine print. See
   eleventy_binary/lib/settings.js, which sorts both lists and falls back to
   nav when a project has not declared a footer list of its own.

   "Back to top" is the last entry in that same row and wears the same
   .nav-link as the rest. It is a bare anchor to #top on <body> — no script:
   the smooth travel is html { scroll-behavior: smooth } in section E of
   input.css, and section I already disables it under prefers-reduced-motion. #}
<footer class="site-footer">
  <div class="shell" style="padding-block:3.5rem 4rem;">
    <hr class="rule-grad" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem;">

    <div class="block-two-col" style="align-items:start;">
      <div>
        {# The name alone. The mark that used to sit beside it now closes the
           footer from the bottom-right corner — see .footer-baseline below —
           so this column is type only, and .text-flow is free to carry the
           gradient without a flat mark beside it to argue with.

           A link home, like the wordmark in the bar, wearing .link-underline
           so the drawn rule is the same one every prose link on the site uses.
           The gradient sits on the <span> INSIDE the anchor and not on the
           anchor itself — both classes paint a background, and one element
           cannot serve both. See .footer-name in input.css.

           No aria-label: the visible name is the accessible name, which is
           what WCAG 2.5.3 asks for and what a voice-control user will say.
           The bar's wordmark needs one because half of it is a picture; this
           is text. Two links to "/" on the page is fine. #}
        {# Kept on one line on purpose: whitespace between <a> and <span> is
           part of the anchor's inline box, and the underline is drawn to that
           box — so a newline in here can hang the rule a space wider than the
           name it belongs to. #}
        <p class="display-sm"><a class="footer-name link-underline" href="/"><span class="text-flow">{{ settings.name }}</span></a></p>
      </div>

      <nav class="footer-nav" aria-label="Footer">
        {% for item in settings.footer_nav %}
        <a class="nav-link" href="{{ item.link }}"{% if page.url == item.link %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a>
        {% endfor %}
        {# Last in the same row, and .nav-link like the rest of them: it is a
           link the reader picks out of the footer cluster, not a control
           bolted onto the copyright line. #}
        <a class="nav-link back-to-top" href="#top"><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↑</span> Back to top</a>
      </nav>
    </div>

    {# The closing line of the footer, and the mark that ends it. The fine
       print runs down the left, the mark sits hard against the shell's right
       edge and is bottom-aligned with the last line of it, so it lands in the
       corner of the page rather than floating beside the text.

       Same file and same mask as the nav mark, at --logo-footer-size. It is
       kept OUTSIDE both <p>s, not tucked into the copyright line: that token
       is in em and resolves against the font-size it is inherited at, so
       inside .micro it would come out at the size of the smallest type on the
       page. Out here it inherits the footer's body size, which is what the
       token was tuned against.

       Still hidden from assistive tech. It is decoration either way, and the
       site name is printed in text further up this same footer. #}
    <div class="footer-baseline">
      <div>
        <p class="micro" style="color:var(--fg-faint);">
          © {{ build.date | year }} {{ settings.author or settings.name }}
        </p>

        {% if settings.footer_note %}
        <p style="margin-top:0.6rem; font-size:var(--step--1); color:var(--fg-muted); max-width:60ch;">{{ settings.footer_note }}</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>

      <span class="site-logo footer-logo" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
